## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical rate-limiting design for multi-tenant LLM gateways, focusing on fairness, latency guarantees, and graceful degradation under burst conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant quotas with burst handling capabilities
- SLA enforcement and backpressure mechanisms
- Comprehensive observability and rollback safety measures

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    return this.tokens >= 1;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical fault-tolerance thinking in multi-tenant LLM ops, focusing on isolation, degradation, and testability.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant SLAs; circuit breakers; graceful degradation
- Fallback models and caching; backoff strategies
- Incident testing with staged outages

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of a simple per-tenant circuit breaker
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(limit=5, timeoutMs=1000){ this.failures=0; this.state='CLOSED'; this.limit=limit; this.timeoutMs=timeoutMs; }
  recordFailure(){ this.failures++; if(this.failures>=this.limit) this.state='OPEN'; }
  reset(){ this.failures=0; this.state='CLOSED'; }
  canProceed(){ return this.state!=='OPEN'; }
}
```

